<Layout title="Research Statement | Himanshu Dixit" activeTab="research">
  <h2>Research Agenda</h2>

  <p>
    Modern deep learning relies heavily on scaling the compute and parameter budget of autoregressive 
    sequence models. While this approach has demonstrated impressive few-shot empirical capabilities, 
    it faces severe theoretical and practical bottlenecks: quadratic attention complexity, context rot, 
    and the lack of persistent internal state.
  </p>
  <p>
    My research investigates alternative foundations for scalable intelligence across three interrelated 
    thrusts: <strong>memory-augmented agent runtimes</strong>, <strong>continuous dynamical systems</strong>, 
    and <strong>high-throughput systems engineering</strong>.
  </p>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <div class="item-block">
    <h3>1. Autonomous Agent Architectures &amp; Hierarchical Memory</h3>
    <p class="item-desc">
      Stateless prompt-response paradigms force language models to reconstruct reasoning trajectories on 
      every step from an increasingly bloated context window. In real-world software engineering and 
      autonomous robotics, tasks require hundreds of sequential decisions over days or weeks.
    </p>
    <p class="item-desc">
      I design deterministic execution runtimes that separate ephemeral reasoning context from 
      permanent procedural memory. By unifying episodic vector embeddings with structural knowledge graphs, 
      agents maintain accurate internal representations of complex codebases and environments without 
      degrading due to attention dilution.
    </p>
    <p class="item-links">
      <a href="https://github.com/Himan-D/agent-memory" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">[Related System: agent-memory]</a>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="item-block">
    <h3>2. Continuous-Time Sequence Models (SSMs) &amp; Dynamical Systems</h3>
    <p class="item-desc">
      Standard Transformer self-attention scales at <em>O</em>(<em>N</em><sup>2</sup>) in both computational operations 
      and memory footprint with respect to sequence length <em>N</em>. Structured State-Space Models (e.g., S4, Mamba) 
      and continuous-time Neural Differential Equations offer a principled alternative: mapping input continuous 
      signals to latent representations in strictly linear or sub-quadratic time.
    </p>
    <p class="item-desc">
      My working papers explore the algebraic properties and stability criteria of discretized state matrices, 
      evaluating their capacity to retain associative recall and hierarchical structure over unconstrained 
      context windows.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="item-block">
    <h3>3. High-Throughput Inference &amp; Memory Paging Systems</h3>
    <p class="item-desc">
      Theoretical algorithmic improvements are meaningless without low-level systems implementations capable of 
      saturating modern accelerator memory bandwidth. 
    </p>
    <p class="item-desc">
      My systems work involves performance characterization on modern GPU architectures: analyzing 
      PagedAttention KV-cache fragmentation, write-amplification in high-concurrency continuous batching, 
      and custom kernel execution in C++/CUDA and PyTorch.
    </p>
    <p class="item-links">
      <a href="https://github.com/Himan-D/vllm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">[Serving Experiments: vllm Fork]</a>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="item-block">
    <h3>4. Topological Graph Learning &amp; Materials Informatics</h3>
    <p class="item-desc">
      Many physical and structural systems do not naturally reside in Euclidean space. In materials informatics 
      and financial micro-structures, symmetry groups and topological connectivity dictate physical properties 
      and market contagion.
    </p>
    <p class="item-desc">
      I build deep learning toolkits and graph neural networks (GNNs) that exploit group equivariance to predict 
      crystalline formation energies and model multi-agent correlation graphs in real-time.
    </p>
    <p class="item-links">
      <a href="https://github.com/Himan-D/matgraph-cli" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">[Tool: matgraph-cli]</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/Himan-D/fingraph" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">[Engine: fingraph]</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
